and you're watching book tv on c-span 2  live coverage of the miami book fair  charlie sykes who you just heard will be  joining us a little bit later and you'll  have the chance to talk with him in  about 15 minutes or so cnn's van jones  will be talking about his new book so  that's coming up  in the meantime we are set up in the  center of the festival with a book tv  call in set  and we're pleased to be joined by  author and journalist jefferson morley  his most recent book is called the ghost  the secret life of cia spymaster james  jesus angleton  who was  mr angleton mr morley james angleton  would be born in 1917 he'd be 100 years  old this year he was a yale educated spy  and one of really the founding  personalities of the cia one of the most  powerful men in the cia from its  founding in 1947  until his forest retirement in 1974. so  really a founding father of of us  counterintelligence what type of issues  and areas did he work on  angleton used his position as chief of  counter intelligence staff to really  create a kind of cia within the cia and  so he he dabbled everywhere uh he was  very influential in u.s policy towards  israel  created programs of mass surveillance  opening the mail of of of americans um  involved in the uh  domestic surveillance schemes of the  nixon white house for example um  involved in the early days of the mk  ultra mind experiment program a story i  tell in the ghost this was a man who  made his influence felt everywhere very  involved in u.s policy towards cuba  so this position he a lot of cia  officers rise through a geographical  directorate you know division within the  cia angleton was kept had his fingers in  many many things was he a well-known  personality during the years he was  active no he was not in fact there's  stories that that  i one story that i heard one man worked  for him for four years and never knew  who he was he was very secretive in his  ways even within the cia  one of the underlying themes of your  book is the importance of bureaucratic  infighting that comes up again and again  yes angleton is a master of that as an  administrator he's kind of lousy he  doesn't deal well with his subordinates  imagine having somebody work for you for  four years and doesn't know who you are  but in a one-to-one meeting angleton was  extremely intelligent and his  presentations were very compelling he  cultivated alan dulles  richard helms cia directors and so he  was able to maintain his position even  when he came in for a lot of criticism  from his other colleagues in the cia so  jefferson morley  if you survive in the cia at that level  from 47 to 75 we often think about j  edgar hoover over at the fbi surviving  that many presidents  did he get close to the presidents as  well or was he did he have dirt on  people what was a he was like j edgar  hoover and he was he was a close  collaborator with hoover for for many  years hoover didn't like the cia at all  but he did come to trust angleton  because angleton supplied him with that  kind of secret information and and both  hoover and angleton were masters at  using secrets to leverage their power so  yes angleton did collect information  the president and the presidents he was  probably closest to president nixon  nixon was the man who appealed to him  most he thought eisenhower may be a bit  stodgy jfk a bit weak lbj a bit crude  nixon for angleton was was the best  prepared president so he liked nixon a  lot and it's it's telling that you know  they both fell  at the same time and for the same reason  because they were spying on their  enemies uh they were obsessive about  secrecy nixon resigns in august of 1974  and angleton is fired in december of  1974. kind of the last chapter of  watergate  is there a room at the cia named after  mr angleton no you know within the cia  angleton is not in in good standing then  when this  they sent people agents back to review  what what had this man done because it  was so secretive the cia themselves  didn't know  and the internal consensus of the cia on  angleton is negative that he made a lot  of mistakes and he and his actions  harmed the agency so  he has not honored  that much within the agency  you say in the book the ghost that his  legacy is the mass surveillance of  americas of americans yeah  you know  today we have these powerful systems of  mass surveillance that we've learned  where the nsa can pick up on your email  and your telephone calls angleton  pioneered an early version of that which  was the opening of the mail of americans  overseas mail  he picked up this program in 1955 it was  very small  uh it was opening 100 200 letters a year  within a few years he was opening 10 000  letters a year and on the barest of  authority there was really no legal  justification there was no warrant there  was no probable cause for opening  people's mail uh and he he ran this  program from 50 late 50s to  the early 70s opening 10 000 letters a  year copying them indexing the names  so this really was america's first mass  surveillance program so you can see it  as the  as the progenitor of the of the of the  systems that we have today  he used the same justification for these  systems  that we hear today from the nsa and the  cia  another line from the book is that mr  angleton was not surprised  when lee harvey oswald shot jfk  well he knew he might have been  surprised when he shot him but he knew  very well who lee harvey oswald was and  one of the most distinctive things but  one of the most important pieces of the  book is that angleton put oswald under  close monitoring in november 1959 and  for the next four years wherever oswald  went angleton was informed in detail  about what he was doing who he was in  touch with his politics his personal  life his foreign travels so  the story that was fed to the american  people after the assassination that this  this guy came out of nowhere and shot  the president that was a cover story to  protect angleton  oswald had been monitored for four years  before november 22 1963. did the cia  know he was in dallas in november of 63.  a week before  the assassination on november 15 1963  angleton's top aide signs for an fbi  report and we have the routing slip jane  roman his liaison officer received the  report and in that report the new  orleans fbi office reported that oswald  had returned from mexico city and was  living in the dallas area so i believe  that angleton read that report he was a  voracious consumer of intelligence  i believe that angleton knew oswald was  in dallas a week before the  assassination now mr morley you've been  a reporter for 30 some years did you  just go out to the cia and go through  their archives to find this information  no i asked them for an interview  and and  not surprisingly they turned me down i  got this book from interviews with  retired cia people declassified cia  records and archival material at the lbj  library the gerald ford library the jfk  library as well as different collections  at stanford yale and uh boston  university so a lot this was kind of an  investigative biography  if the cia  documents were available would this be a  different book or  absolutely there's a secret study of of  angleton's tenure as chief of the  counterintelligence staff it's 12  volumes each volume is three to four  hundred pages and that study  which encompasses angleton's entire  career is totally classified so  the the the full story of james angleton  is still classified this as i say in the  beginning of the book this is the story  as best it can be told so far  if americans are suspicious about a  shadow  government  running this country  your book gives a little bit of support  to that doesn't it well you know when i  started this book nobody talked about  the deep state that was an unknown  phrase two years later the president of  the united states believes in it and  it's in headlines all the time so this  suspicion is very deep so i i wasn't  going to look for the deep state but if  you look at angleton's career you see  the power that somebody can  accumulate  operating within these secret agencies  and it does lend credence to these fears  that people are secretly manipulating  our political system from these  positions of secrecy angleton certainly  did  what do we not know about him in his or  what what should we know about his  personal life where did he live in  washington who were his friends  engleton was a very um attractive man  personally and people really  you know people really believed in him  um he was a father of three he lived  very modestly in uh in arlington  virginia off of glebe road um his his  wife came from a very wealthy family but  there was nothing ostentatious about him  he was really an intellectual he he  cared about books and travel  he was at the end of his career an  alcoholic and that tended to isolate him  and and it also made his thinking rigid  and this is what led to his downfall he  lost allies within the cia by the early  1970s but you know what even when i  didn't like him he was a very  he was never an uninteresting man he was  a very complex  character and he was always interesting  to try and figure out  how he how he was thinking and how he  made his way  he was in his office until 1975. have  you talked with people who knew him well  yes i did um i talked with a man named  ephraim halevi who was the  uh eventually became the chief of the  mossad  and um halevi knew angleton from when  angleton would go to israel which he did  quite frequently  and halevi was a junior officer who was  you know carrying his briefcase and  driving the car  um but halevi knew him quite well i  talked with a man named peter sischel  who was a  cia officer who worked with him in the  1950s  i talked with jane roman who was his  aide bill hood was another man who  worked for him who i interviewed so  these retired cia people  some of whom are now dead  you know provided this insight into what  was the man like in his work  jefferson morley spent many years at the  washington post  and uh do you write for them anymore  uh occasionally and do you have a piece  coming up yes i'm going to write about  the jfk files in the washington post the  ones that were just recently released  yeah we're trying to go through them and  figure out what's new and significant  and i have some ideas towards that end  have you found and you're going through  these what 28 000 pages or files  have you found some new material  yeah there are things that are new it's  a little bit confusing because some of  this stuff has been made public before  and people who come to the subject for  the first time think oh wow look at this  those of us who've been  involved in this you know know that  that's not true but so far there have  been some interesting things but  remember the cia knows what's in there  so they're not going to release the good  stuff first they're going to release the  good stuff last so we are still waiting  for the most important material to be  released the author is jefferson morley  the book the ghost the secret life of  cia spymaster  james jesus angleton  book tv is live in miami for the miami  book fair and coming up next is cnn's  van jones his new book is called beyond  the messy truth he's going to be talking  about that afterwards we're going to  show you a little bit of the book  signing that mr jones is doing and then  we want to hear your reaction to what he  has to say in his presentation so it's  your chance to talk about what you heard  and that's what's coming up several more  hours live  go to our website booktv.org to see the  full schedule and beginning in just a  moment van jones  you

